`HOW-IT-WORKS.md` in the repo root explains the domain model and the rules the
app turns on. Read it when you need the reasoning behind something here.

# There is no server

Everything runs in the browser against Firestore. Prefer deriving state on read
over persisting it: a stored "missed" or "overdue" record would have to be
written by whichever tab happened to be open at midnight, so several open tabs
race and a household that never opens the app leaves holes. See the comment on
`missedOccurrences` in `lib/schedule-view.ts`.

# One source of truth per concern

| Concern | Render through | Never |
|---|---|---|
| Day list and week grid | `buildScheduleRows` in `lib/schedule-view.ts` | Assemble a view from `Chore` directly — the two views drifted apart this way |
| Who did what | `chore.completions` | The activity log |
| A person's icon | `components/Avatar.tsx` | A hand-rolled circle with `name.charAt(0)` |

Resolve a photo with `resolvePhoto` in `app/page.tsx`, which falls back to the
Google account picture when the Firestore document has none, and hand the result
to `Avatar` (or down to a child component) as `photoURL`.

`chore.completions` is keyed `YYYY-MM-DD` and holds `{ userId, at, skipped?,
cancelled? }`. A `skipped` day handed the turn on and is still owed; a
`cancelled` day was written off. Neither is work anybody did.

The activity log is the wrong source for statistics: it is capped at
`LOG_FEED_LIMIT` records with no date filter, and it is append-only, so undoing
a completion writes a second record rather than removing the first.

# Adding a field to a chore

`firestore.rules` validates chore documents by exact key count. A new field
needs three edits or writes silently start failing:

1. the `hasX` line and the `totalSize` sum in `isValidChoreStrict`
2. a type/size check for the field
3. a case in `scripts/rules.test.ts`

Fields written before a change must keep working — treat a missing field as the
old behaviour, the way a missing `startDate` means the schedule has no lower
bound.

# Tests

Pure logic goes in `lib/`, its test in `scripts/<name>.test.ts` using
`node:assert/strict`, registered in the `test:unit` script in `package.json`.
Assert behaviour a user would notice, and say why in the message.
